{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqq0vwgsq1k","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Unjudged autowire edge means node not ready — JUDGING gate drops its timeout; CLI judge is the on-demand un-gate","summary":"DECISION (2026-06-22, user-directed): the JUDGING->READY gate becomes STRICT and loses its time-based auto-release. INVARIANT: a node with ANY unjudged autowire candidate edge is NOT READY until those edges are judged (keep/prune) — remove JUDGING_TIMEOUT_MS (soft ~10min, lib/judge.js:~465) and JUDGING_HARD_CEILING_MS (hard ~30min, ~484); the 409 claim gate at routes/overlay.js:466-471 no longer has a timeout exception. RATIONALE: the timeout was only a backstop against the slow/hang-prone ASYNC background judge. Now that judging is fast + synchronous (P1 sync judge-drain reusing runJudgeLoop) and can be driven ON DEMAND via a CLI (node scripts/judge-drain-once.js --node <key>), the timeout is vestigial. The CLI judge REPLACES the timeout as the escape hatch: a gated node is un-gated by actually judging its edges, not by waiting out a clock. SAFETY: no infinite-stuck risk — both the eager judge (auto on node-add) and the CLI (manual) can drain a gated node. This REFINES the JUDGING-gate detail of the floor-drop decision (note-mqq0q02xylw said 'until judged OR timed-out'; the 'or timed-out' branch is now removed). Shipped as feature orch/feature/bench-prod-parity task P6 (codex/p6-strict-judging-gate), gated on P1.","category":null,"tags":["judging-gate","eager-judge","daemon","parity","feature:bench-prod-parity"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-23T02:25:59.836Z","vec":[-0.0920184850692749,0.020559120923280716,-0.06051556393504143,0.021749082952737808,0.05674021318554878,-0.03402459993958473,-0.020988289266824722,0.01715424284338951,0.004375729244202375,0.011529686860740185,0.010500198230147362,0.03436050936579704,-0.02768530510365963,-0.03930617868900299,-0.04517881199717522,0.04334890842437744,0.104803167283535,-0.0656098797917366,-0.08358453214168549,-0